he player perspective  that's where more the frustration is um  i think there's a stive there's  definitely a sigh of relief  for  the teams and the owners and everything  like that who now  don't have to go through a three-week  break  don't have to go through a three-week  break in this season and they get a  little bit of that flexibility to try  and make up some of these lost games it  won't be the easiest thing to do it's  definitely going to be quite the  juggling act because  rescheduling games is never the easiest  thing but the owners and and the owners  and the teams  bit relieved today definitely really  does this all came out players obviously  frustrated but yeah i think at the end  of the day players were also getting to  the point of  they started to wonder if it was worth  it as well which is why ultimately it  became a joint decision from the league  and the players association which i  think is important to remember on this  it wasn't as simple  so and i imagine rescheduling the games  when you share an arena is also going to  be a challenge as well for a lot of  these teams  are owners in the league being  unnecessarily short-sighted when they  see the olympics as intrusive and a risk  to their business i mean it does  basically spread the sport of hockey  build up that good will with viewers  around the world  yeah it's kind of it's kind of a  just i guess it's you kind of weigh  different options there for on one hand  if you look from the owner perspective  and completely justified anything about  is  if you're going to take or we do we  really want to take a three-week break  for games that are essentially going to  be played at three in the morning  um the three in the morning are our time  and well obviously it's great for the  game and it's great for to see these  best on best tournaments from a business  standpoint i don't know how much  uh the owner of the  wings or the tampa bay lightning get  from  letting their players go playing a game  that's played at 3m local time um i i  i've always felt that  there's  there there should be the olympic kind  of best on best set up there is great  for the sport i think it's created some  great memories  i think from a league perspective i  think this is the type of thing that  also leads to some other conversations  that they should probably have with the  international olympic committee that  would allow them to work better in  tandem because one thing i think that we  often forget about is the ioc is  incredibly restrictive with their rights  and who and and everything like that so  every one of the big one of the best  moments in  hockey history is sydney crosby's golden  goal in vancouver the nhl does not have  the right it does not have the rights  from the ioc to use that clip to promote  you can't walk and so it's little things  like that where i certainly understand  where the nhl comes from and that  perspective of if the ioc is not going  to  kind of give a little bit back to make  it to help better promote your game  why are you going to basically promote  their events so i think it's kind of  it's more nuanced than  should they or shouldn't they go it's  how do we go in the future and they  definitely they will go in 2020 2026 the  players um  in the league have that agreement but as  they move forward i'm sure the nhl and  the ioc will want to have some  conversations how can we actually work  in tandem on this because yeah right now  it's that's that's always been kind of  one of the bigger issues to me is they  don't promote each other enough beyond  just the fact it's hockey yeah they're  kind of working at cross purposes and i  i did not realize that idea that they  didn't have the rights to some of those  highlights that's really interesting  something else that you've been writing  about on the athletic sean is how the  nhl corporate business has really boomed  and lifted the league  during the pandemic why is hockey  related revenue on track to reach 5.2  billion dollars this season that that  seems incredible to me  yeah and it's i mean it's even more  incredible when you think about it that  that's above the pre pre-pandemic number  5.1 from the last 5.1 billion from the  last season um hockey has always been a  gate driven league and by great drive  and i mean  more than  about 60 percent of our revenue was  coming from tickets food and beverage at  games basically um revenue related to  people at the game and one of the things  that's really taken a step forward for  the league within the last two within  the last 12 months specifically is how  much corporate business has really  invested in the sport and one of the  biggest flash points  for that was the new nhl tv deal with  espn and turner sports they went from a  10-year deal with nbc's with nbc  universal to  um the two national tv deals with turner  and the walt disney company and that was  kind of a um  that kind of raised the  value of the nhl if you're if you're  looking at if you're looking at sponsors  like for example one that recently uh  partnered with the nhl is a comp is  chipotle  i just know this because chipotle is not  sponsored with any other pro sports like  part of the reason was that was they  looked at the fact that the nhl is on  espn the nhl is on turner they looked at  it as this valuations going there and  then the other thing that's really that  that people people are trying to be  smarter and savvier with their money and  they are also noticing now that  it is still evil prices and demand have  gone up to partner with the nhl it still  is on average considerably less  expensive for an advertiser or a  corporate sponsor to  promote to to sponsor something in hl  compared to the mba interesting  yeah so  so they're seeing so brands are seeing  a much larger return of investment well  the mba may have more viewers or the nfl  definitely has more viewers  you may not be reaching the same amount  of people but the cost the cost that  people the cost that brands are seeing  by getting with the nhl compared to  having to pay more with the other ones  they're starting to see okay this is  this is a smart business decision the  nhl is kind of riding that wave right  now okay so it's a return on investment  and relative value compared to some of  the more popular leagues  still at the same time when it comes to  this new contract this new streaming  deal with espn it can be complicated for  for fans who don't attend the game in  person to be able to find the right game  it's not often shown on espn when it is  on espn plus that's an added layer of  complication have the new streaming  rights made things overly complicated  for viewers  it's definitely  if you are a traditional north american  sports fan it is kind of been a bit of a  sticker shock type moment where  games on esp oh well it's a national tv  game when really it's a national  streamed game um and so it's kind of  been a bit of a sticker shock for the  traditional north american fan um in  general though it's kind of where we're  going to be going with sports deals in  the future we've seen it within the  united states you look at the english  premier league syria you look at  international soccer leagues and so much  of that is built around these streaming  deals and the espn nhl deal is kind of  the first foray into a north american  domestic league that's really going into  that um and even the new nfl deal that  that  they sign which i believe starts in two  years as i believe when it starts but  there's going to be an exclusive amazon  game and people are going to be have to  to stream to watch that nfl game we'll  have to get amazon prime or whatever  whichever one it is yeah um so this it's  kind of it's definitely um  led to some complications and  frustrations there's there definitely  could have been much better messaging  from the league in the teams i believe  in better communicating this but at the  end of the 